# EMI Coach Engineering Principles

- This is a financial application. Correctness, privacy, auditability and deterministic calculations have priority over speed of implementation.
- Use React Native + TypeScript for shared UI and domain orchestration.
- Isolate Android/iOS-specific behavior behind native modules or adapters.
- Keep detailed personal financial records local on the device by default.
- All user-facing financial calculations must be implemented in deterministic TypeScript packages (`amortization-engine`, `transaction-engine`, `cashflow-engine`, `debt-coach-engine`, `credit-engine`, `financial-state`).
- Python must NOT calculate EMI, interest, amortization, debt-free dates, balance-transfer savings, cashflow metrics, or credit-card repayment math. Python is for backend metadata only.
- Do not use an LLM to calculate money.
- No financial ledger data may be stored on the backend. Do not introduce a backend transaction-ledger endpoint unless explicitly approved by an architecture document.
- Screens may not import financial engine packages. `FinancialState` (`packages/financial-state`) is the only read model for dashboards and planning.
- Never collect or persist bank passwords, UPI PINs, debit/ATM PINs, CVV, full PAN, or equivalent credentials.
- External financial providers must be behind adapter interfaces. No vendor SDK is added without verification documentation in `docs/integrations/`.
- Any new data field must identify: source, sensitivity, retention, local/server boundary and deletion behavior.
- Never log sensitive financial payloads.
- Use migrations for all persistent-schema changes.
- Prefer small composable modules over large screens/services.
- Every financial feature must include happy-path and edge-case tests.
- Never silently change accounting semantics. In particular, transfers and debt repayments must not be counted as spending twice.
